.TH UBERTOOTH 7 "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "Project Ubertooth Overview"
.SH NAME
.PP
.BR ubertooth (7) 
\- Project Ubertooth
.SH OVERVIEW
.PP
Ubertooth is a platform for Bluetooth experimentation. It is able to
sniff Bluetooth Smart (BLE), discover undiscoverable classic Bluetooth
devices, and perform rudimentary sniffing of classic Bluetooth devices.
.PP
Ubertooth is \fBnot\fP a Bluetooth dongle and it will not show up as one
when running \fB\fChciconfig\fR\&.
.SH COMMAND LINE TOOLS
.PP
The following command line tools included in this package are the
primary means of using Ubertooth. Refer to their manual pages for
example usage and comprehensive documentation.
.PP
Useful commands:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-btle (1) 
: BLE sniffing and other fun
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-rx (1) 
: Classic Bluetooth device discovery and rudimentary sniffing
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-scan (1) 
: Active scanning of undiscoverable devices
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-afh (1) 
: Detecting the AFH map of a Classic Bluetooth piconet
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-ego (1) 
: Yuneec E\-Go electric skateboard sniffing
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-follow (1) 
: Active (BlueZ) clock discovery and piconet following
.RE
.PP
Utility commands:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-dfu (1) 
: Firmware update tool
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-dump (1) 
: Dumping raw RF symbols to disk
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-tx (1) 
: Retransmitting symbols dumped with ubertooth\-dump
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-ducky (1) 
: Driving an Uberducky as a BLE USB Rubber Ducky
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-util (1) 
: "Everything else"
.RE
.PP
Less useful commands:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-debug (1) 
: Peeking and poking registers on the CC2400
.IP \(bu 2
.BR ubertooth-specan (1) 
: Raw RSSI values used by graphical specan
.RE
.SH OPTION VALUES
.PP
These pages write an option's value as a separate word: \fB\fCubertooth\-util
\-c 2440\fR, \fB\fCubertooth\-btle \-a 8e89bed6\fR, \fB\fCubertooth\-rx \-l
446688 \-u 22\fR\&. This follows a standard, the POSIX Utility Syntax
Guidelines (guideline 6: an option and its value are separate arguments). The
value placeholders are written the same way (\fB\fC\-c <2402\-2480>\fR). The
older attached form is still accepted for backward compatibility with existing
scripts, so \fB\fC\-c 2440\fR and
\fB\fC\-c2440\fR are the same. Where the value is optional, the next word is
taken as the value only when it is a valid value, so an option never swallows
the option after it: \fB\fC\-c \-b\fR reads the channel and then the board ID,
while \fB\fC\-z \-90\fR sets the squelch to \-90 dBm. A long option takes
\fB\fC\-\-name value\fR or \fB\fC\-\-name=value\fR, never attached without the
\fB\fC=\fR (no long option takes a value today; this is for consistency with
\fB\fCgetopt_long\fR). A lone \fB\fC\-\fR as the value of a file option means
standard input or standard output, so \fB\fCubertooth\-btle \-q \-\fR writes
the capture to stdout.
.SH SELECTING A DEVICE
.PP
With a single Ubertooth attached, the tools find it automatically. With more
than one attached, every tool takes \fB\fC\-U <index|serial>\fR to choose one:
an index is a position in the list of attached Ubertooths (\fB\fC\-U 0\fR is
the first), and a serial\-number string selects that board whatever the enumeration
order. An index that is not attached is refused
(\fB\fCerror: no Ubertooth at index 2 (2 attached)\fR) and the tool exits
non\-zero. Indexes are positions in the list of boards attached when the
command runs, so they shift when a board is added or removed; to keep
targeting the same board, select it by serial.
.PP
A serial may be shortened to its first few characters, in either case, so
\fB\fC\-U 1cc0\fR and \fB\fC\-U 1CC0\fR select the same board. If those
characters start more than one attached board's serial, the tool lists the
matching serials and selects neither. An all\-digits string is read as an
index, so include a non\-digit (\fB\fC\-U 05f\fR rather than \fB\fC\-U 05\fR). See
.BR ubertooth-util (1),
SELECTING A DEVICE, for the full description and for listing the attached
devices.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.PP
\fB\fCUBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES=1\fR makes every tool behave as if no Ubertooth
were attached: nothing is listed or opened on the USB bus, and the tool reports
that it found no device. It exists for the project's own test suite, which runs
the tools while boards may be in use elsewhere, and it is the only setting that
changes device selection. Only the exact value \fB\fC1\fR turns it on; unset,
empty or any other value leaves device selection alone. A tool that finds no
board because of it says so:
\fB\fCnote: UBERTOOTH_TEST_NO_DEVICES is set, so no board is selected\fR.
